28 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186281556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372209 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186372216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133928 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133928 #edb/ref 1039 "Evaluating a Surface Integral Using Stokes' Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133928 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133928 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "d65a039f-6022-547d-a852-c249da5bd8ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1045 "Stokes' Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1050 1380.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134013 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e33ca737-353c-5d82-bb61-fdad7272cf57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134130 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134130 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161926" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134130 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134130 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2y⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134130 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ee52e831-6d2f-5771-b93b-7f7fa07dc36b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134130 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=4-x-2y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=4-x-2y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-x-2y)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,-2y⟩{|}_{z=4-x-2y} \\\\  & =⟨{{0}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{-2y}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-0\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-2)+(-2y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-palne is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-2y\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}2r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot [{r}^{2}]_{r=0}^{r=1} \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134134 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134134 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134134 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134134 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ for $t\\in [-1,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=x\\,i+y\\,k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134134 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "008f670b-b77a-5939-9c4f-8602d138cc7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134134 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t),\\,z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t).\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t))\\,k \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,i-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,j+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)-0\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi {cos}^{2}⁡(\\pi t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi t)))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\pi t)}{2}+\\frac{\\pi t}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\pi t)}{4}]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0-(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134927 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134927 #edb/ref 1061 44631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134927 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82a78598-8d2f-5d8f-8ae3-e776f9d83475" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134927 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134928 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134928 #edb/ref 1061 44632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134928 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88e66c3b-74ca-5e04-acec-166bd6c75812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134928 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134929 #edb/ref 1061 44635 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134929 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8a1dabb2-c1a5-5219-ad30-80f6604e07d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134929 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134930 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134930 #edb/ref 1061 44682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134930 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "852f8019-128f-5411-864f-ac77ce109b26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134930 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134931 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134929 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134931 #edb/ref 1061 44684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134931 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b2a1a0ff-e72c-5813-8d4e-f400e76cbbcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134931 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134932 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134932 #edb/ref 1061 44722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134932 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f2180bbb-1f4c-5c73-b6d2-6adfe5adde9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134932 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134937 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134937 #edb/ref 1061 44883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "66ae9e5c-30ea-595a-becb-39e1f2dc5651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134938 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134938 #edb/ref 1061 44885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134938 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "367db095-5578-5dfe-813e-342c9188b902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134938 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135161 #edb/ref 1064 "To understand the following work, it's a good idea to review Green's theorem.\n\nSuppose $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, and simple closed curve in $\\Bbb R^2,$ and $D$ is the region enclosed by $C.$ If we consider the two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = \\langle P, Q\\rangle$ to have zero $z$-component, i.e.,  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle P,\\: Q,\\:0\\rangle\n$$\n\nand the scalar functions $P$ and $Q$ have continuous partial derivatives in the interior of $D,$ then Green's theorem can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{k} \\: \\textrm{d}A  = \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\n \n\nGreen's theorem relates the sum of the microscopic curls of $\\mathbf{F}$ over $D$ to the macroscopic curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ (i.e., the circulation) along the boundary of $D.$\n\n![](images/78/7837a682bfbf9d39ab03e42edee33306b53bf66be2fa11f91d2cdfa62ca9e9ac.png)\n\nGreen's theorem is a special case of another important theorem, which we'll discuss shortly. But first, we need to look at the relationship between oriented surfaces and their bounding curves." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135161 #edb/ref 1065 7690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135161 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9eead752-f633-5bf3-a747-29ce56955373" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135161 #edb/ref 1067 "A Review of Green's Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135162 #edb/ref 1064 "Calculating the circulation of a vector field along a closed, piecewise smooth curve $C$ can be time-consuming because it often requires splitting $C$ into several parts, evaluating a line integral along each one, and calculating their sum. \n\nHowever, in such cases, we can often use Stokes' theorem to reduce the amount of work required by converting this sum of line integrals to a single surface integral. \n\nFor example, suppose that $C$ is the intersection of the paraboloid $z=x^2 + y^2$ and the boundary of the infinite vertical column enclosed by the planes $x = \\pm 1$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ Suppose also that $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above, and \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\bigg\\langle 0, \\: \\dfrac{1}{2}z^2, \\: xz \\bigg\\rangle.\n$$\n\nLet's use Stokes' theorem to find the circulation of $\\mathbf{F}$ along $C$.\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n= \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy computing the curl of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n![](images/fd/fd25c80fd757477b724b320d1941c57630ec1c64f36bdb44d4c1c70c526fa2a9.svg)\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into expression for $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-z,\\,-z,\\,0⟩{|}_{z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =⟨{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{P},\\,{{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{Q},\\,{{0}_{⏟}}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr & ={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2x+({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 2y+0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcf7ba14ac2c0f3da12efed6d539817ab1708273f5e8e665fdde14fee28bb4e3.png)\n\nEvaluating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n2\\int_{-1}^{1} \\int_{-1}^{1} x^3 + x^2 y + x y^2 + y^3 \\: \\textrm{d}y  \\: \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = 0.\n$$\n\n**Note**: Stokes theorem tells us that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$\n\n depends only on the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary of $S.$ Therefore, we could have evaluated our line integral by selecting *any* surface with $C$ as its bounding curve and integrating $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ over this surface." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135162 #edb/ref 1065 7700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135162 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe8c7f62-0786-58b3-874d-2aca76ef3ad7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135162 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Stokes' Theorem to Evaluate Line Integrals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135163 #edb/ref 1064 "We're now ready to state the main result of the lesson: \n\n*Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then* \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nThis result is known as **Stokes' theorem** and can be considered a generalization of Green's theorem. Let's spend some time building some intuition behind this result.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field in $\\mathbb R^3.$ Stokes' theorem states that the sum of the microscopic curls of $\\mathbf F$ on the surface $S$ equals the macroscopic curl of $\\mathbf F$ at the boundary $C$ of $S.$ \n\n![](images/30/308aec6213f8dd42c4f853078c0305901bc19568bfd5132221bd72743f4aa58a.png)\n\nConsider the left-hand side of the above picture, and suppose we have a point $P$ that lies on $S.$ \n\nThe magnitude of the flux of $\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F$ through $S$ at $P$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf n.\n$$\n\nWe can think of this quantity as the amount of \"swirl\" of $\\mathbf F$ at $P$ that acts tangentially to $S$ at $P.$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the flux of the curl through $S$ at every point over the entire surface $S,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf n\\, \\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhich we usually write as\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}.\n$$\n\nThis expression gives us the left-hand side of Stokes' theorem.\n\nLet's now look at the right-hand side of our image. The macroscopic curl is simply the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ along the boundary of $S{:}$\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nEquating the sum of the microscopic curls with the circulation, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nStokes' theorem can be summarized in a single picture as follows:\n\n![](images/34/34b39be1166c0556eaad00a473684916c0725c186829f6085db1a322da29612a.png)\n\nWe can immediately deduce a remarkable fact. Notice the value of the surface integral depends only on the circulation at the boundary. Therefore, within a given vector field, if two surfaces $S_1$ and $S_2$ have the same boundary $C,$ then their respective surface integrals will be equal!\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S_1} \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_{S_2} \\textrm{curl}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135163 #edb/ref 1065 7709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135163 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f145e410-13a4-5cdd-ad1d-ba95177d8355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135163 #edb/ref 1067 "Stokes' Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135168 #edb/ref 1064 "The **circulation** $\\Gamma$ of a three-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)$ along a closed, piecewise-smooth curve $C \\in \\mathbb R^3$ parametrized by $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThis is the same definition as the two-dimensional case. Also, like the two-dimensional case, $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a closed curve $C,$ traversed once.\n\nRemember that circulation is always measured with respect to our chosen orientation for $C$ (positive or negative).\n\nLet's get some practice at computing circulation over three-dimensional curves." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135168 #edb/ref 1065 7754 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135168 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fa33c72b-b304-5717-a1ca-c81df764530f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135168 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135169 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have an oriented surface $S$ (not closed) whose **boundary curve** is the closed curve $C,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/bc/bc0ac6b400535839d3571d61c07f3ac752bc4e85020f9b3e4cc08410b0023822.png)\n\nWhenever we select an orientation for $S,$ our chosen orientation automatically induces a positive orientation for $C.$\n\nFor example, if we select the upward-pointing unit normal vector shown below, the positive orientation is *counterclockwise* when viewed from above.\n\n![](images/73/7353d1a742e374e3704155e8c99cc26c62e8486f3606cba1144c35871ad3c432.png)\n\nAssume that you are standing on the curve $C$ with your head pointing upward (i.e., in the same direction as $\\mathbf n$). Then, if you traverse $C$ in the positive direction, the surface will always be on your left.\n\nFor example, if $z=f(x,y)$ is upward-oriented, the projection of the bounding curve onto the $xy$-plane runs counterclockwise when viewed from above. \n\n![](images/e9/e948c13c040a01ce46b6f715e313c8aabb2634e5871739169dd3f38e3229e768.png)\n\nNow, suppose we select the opposite orientation for $\\mathbf n,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/81/81d5e3126c07f30b68bd2d46c4104212f6c7edf34baf65b077ad297f224ebe8a.png)\n\nBy choosing the *opposite* orientation for $\\mathbf n,$ the positive orientation for $C$ is *clockwise* when viewed from above!\n\nIf you're not convinced, grab a child's doll, a toothbrush, or any other object with some kind of head, and mark its left and right sides (be careful not to damage it!). Then, walk it around the edge of a table so that its left side is always next to the table surface. If you turn it upside down, you will see that the object must traverse the table's edge in the *opposite* direction for the table surface to stay on the left." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135169 #edb/ref 1065 7756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135169 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c513ef57-b0ec-554e-9f32-2c13aa08b00c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135169 #edb/ref 1067 "Induced Orientations of Boundary Curves" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428665 #edb/ref 1030 0.5 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428665 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account multivariable: 1 rises / 2 eligible passed source activities = 0.500000; nearest selected fraction = 1/2. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: multivariable=1/2 (1/2). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret co